
******************************************************************************
Modular codebase, with separate layers for:
services/detection/ object-detection backends (YOLOv8, traditional CV and traces
                    of Roboflow API)
services/rules/     rule-based safety logic.
core/config/        tunable configuration parameters.
models/             Pydantic schemas for standardized API responses.
******************************************************************************
Detection
Mainly YOLOv8 for people and vehicles: fast, lightweight. I tested Roboflow to
detect vests but for time constraints. I didn't get to fix some bugs. The design
was adjusted so that vest presence can instead be inferred directly from pixel color.
******************************************************************************
Two alternative PPE rules were implemented:
1)VestRule – checks that a detected vest overlaps a person’s torso (geometry-based).
2)VestColorRule – verifies the presence of orange/yellow pixels in the torso region.
This rule remains active now that the Roboflow backend has been disabled.
An additional RestrictedAreaRule was included but not fully developed.
******************************************************************************
Risk scoring
Each breach contributes a weighted value to an overall risk score (0–10),
defined in config.py for easy tuning.
******************************************************************************
Configurability
All thresholds (HSV bounds, torso fractions, IoU coverage, etc.) can be overridden
through environment variables for experimentation or deployment flexibility.
******************************************************************************
What worked well
YOLOv8 integration is fast, stable, and portable even inside Docker.
Color segmentation reliably detects bright orange/yellow vests under
typical lighting. (needs more tests)
Codebase separation (FastAPI + services + models) produced clean maintainability
and clear evaluation paths.
******************************************************************************
What did not work well
The Roboflow model endpoint frequently returned different not-well
documented errors, and of course the alternative of color-based rules is sensitive
to shadows or non-standard vest colors.
******************************************************************************
Additional Dependencies and Reasons
Dependency          Purpose
ultralytics	        Provides the YOLOv8 object-detection model and inference pipeline.
opencv-python 	    Plenty of cv essentials
numpy	            Numerical operations for masks
Pillow (PIL)	    Reading/writing images and resizing
requests	        used to call Roboflow API; still kept for completeness.
fastapi / pydantic	Web API framework
******************************************************************************
Run the service locally:
uv run uvicorn inviol_image_analyser_assignment.app:app --reload --host 0.0.0.0 --port 8000

Health Check
curl -X GET http://localhost:8000/healthcheck

Analyse an Image
curl -X POST "http://localhost:8000/analyse" \
  -F "file=@sample_images/vest_off.png"

Example response
{
  "risk": {"overall": 7, "by_rule": {"ppe_vest_color": 7}},
  "breaches": [
    {
      "rule_id": "ppe_vest_color",
      "severity": "high",
      "message": "Person not wearing visible high-vis vest",
      "subjects": [0]
    }
  ]
}
